<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cotentin Peninsula</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">Cotentin</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cotentin Peninsula is a peninsula of Normandy in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, projecting into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a>, with the port of Cherbourg at its tip. In Gaulish and Roman times it was held by the Unelli, with the Abrincatui to the south. It was granted to the Breton ruler Salomon in 867 and annexed by the dukes of Normandy in 933.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cotentin-peninsula.jpg" alt="Cotentin Peninsula" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eric Gaba (Sting - fr:Sting) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manche_department_relief_location_map_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The peninsula lies wholly within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Manche. To its west lie the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-malo">Saint-Malo</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/channel-islands">Channel Islands</a>, and to the southwest the peninsula of Brittany. It is part of the Armorican Massif, apart from the Plain, which lies in the Paris Basin, and it falls into three areas: the headland of Cap de la Hague, the Cotentin Pass and the valley of the Saire River.</p><p>The oldest stone in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> is found in outcrops on the coast of Cap de la Hague. Some historical documents call the northern part an island, because a broad marshy area that flooded separated it from the mainland, and only a small strip of land in the heath of Lessay joined them. Gates at river outlets on the west coast and in the Baie des Veys later made it a true peninsula.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Roman times the peninsula belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armorica">Armorica</a>. The town now called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coutances">Coutances</a>, capital of the Gaulish Unelli, took the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamis-cyprus">Constantia</a> in 298 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-i">Constantius Chlorus</a>. The base of the peninsula, the pagus Constantinus, joined the pagus Coriovallensis around Cherbourg and became known as the Cotentin. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a> it was administered by viscounts of the Saint-Sauveur family.</p><p>After Frankish power collapsed, Norse settlers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> region, of Norwegian origin, settled the peninsula heavily. Place names such as La Hague, La Hougue and those ending in -tot and -bec derive from Old Norse, and the pagi of Haga, Sarnes and Helganes bore Norse names as late as 1027.</p><p>In 933 the peninsula passed from Brittany to the Duchy of Normandy, and there was at first much hostility between the new Danish overlords and the Norwegian settlers, including a pagan rebellion against Longsword's rule. In 1088 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-curthose">Robert Curthose</a>, Duke of Normandy, gave the Cotentin to his brother Henry, who later became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and made it his first power base.</p><p>During the Hundred Years War Edward III of England landed in the bay of La Hougue and knighted his son Edward, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-black-prince">Black Prince</a>, at Quettehou. The naval Battle of La Hogue was fought in 1692. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the westernmost of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normandy-landings">D-Day</a> landings came at Utah Beach, followed by a campaign to take Cherbourg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The peninsula has about 250,000 inhabitants. Its largest town, Cherbourg-en-Cotentin, is a cross-channel port and shipbuilding centre. Agriculture is the main economic resource, and the La Hague plant reprocesses nuclear fuel. The local Norman dialect, Cotentinais, survives, and the painter Jean-François Millet was born on the peninsula.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cotentin Peninsula” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
